|!Branch|!Color (palette order)|!Role|
|main|git0 — default primary|Production-ready code; tagged releases live here|
|develop|git1 — default secondary|Integration branch for completed features|
|feature/user-auth|git2 — 3rd in cycle|Feature work branched from develop|
|feature/dashboard|git3 — 4th in cycle|Second feature branch|
|release/1.0|git4 — 5th in cycle|Release-prep branch branched from develop|
|hotfix/auth-crash|git5 — 6th in cycle|Emergency fix branched from main|

---

!! Syntax

```
gitGraph
    commit id: "Label" tag: "v1.0" type: HIGHLIGHT
    branch branchName
    checkout branchName
    commit
    checkout main
    merge branchName id: "Merge label"
    cherry-pick id: "commit-id"
```

|!Commit type|!Meaning|
|`NORMAL`|Default commit dot|
|`REVERSE`|Shows a cross (reverted)|
|`HIGHLIGHT`|Filled circle (release or milestone)|

!! Tips

* Branch colors are assigned automatically in order (`git0` through `git7`) as each new branch is created — you cannot set hex colors per branch in the diagram source without `themeVariables` in the global config.
* Use `id: "..."` on commits to give meaningful labels; without it commits show a short hash.
* `tag: "v1.0"` adds a version badge above the commit.
* `cherry-pick id: "commit-id"` replicates a specific commit to the current branch.
* See also: [[Flowchart]] for deployment pipelines, [[State Diagram]] for release state machines.

